00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.290
So here's what the next 45
minutes or so will be about.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.440
First, I'm going to give an
introduction to containers

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.530
and what they mean for,
say, a .NET developer.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.540
How we can build
these containerized

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.720
apps for the Google
Cloud Platform.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:11.440
And then go into specifically
the Google App Engine

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.810
flexible environment and
Google Container Engine

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.400
for running these
containers on the cloud.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.060
And finally, show all
of the wonderful things

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.490
that Stackdriver
diagnostics can allow

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:26.280
you to see in terms of
insights in your application.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.550
So let's start with
some wonderful books

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.700
you can find on our website.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.505
You know, "What is a Container"
or "Stress-free deployment with

00:05:34.505 --> 00:05:35.970
Containers".

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.860
The simplest way
to break this down

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.630
is-- think of a container
as a way to deploy software.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.840
If you have a lot of experience
with distributed applications,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.200
maybe you used Xcopy, maybe
you build Windows Installers,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.860
maybe you just use two-gigabyte
virtual image disks

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.540
and ship those around.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.270
Well, that's kind of a
problem and can be a pain.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.040
It's tedious, error prone.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.500
And if you want to
really talk about scaling

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.110
your application,
replicating extra copies,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.950
maybe have one, 10, 1,000
versions of that running

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.470
across various
machines, you need

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.220
to have a consistent
and reliable mechanism

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.200
for packaging up your software.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.210
And that's what a container is.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.100
It's a way to
deploy your software

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.410
and describe it in such
a way that it can easily

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:26.100
be moved around and ran
on different machines

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.600
without needing to know a
lot about the target machine.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.250
Now, there some
interesting concepts,

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.580
such as the OS level
implementation,

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:40.170
or virtualization, or some of
the small isolation or security

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:40.950
benefits.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.440
But for this talk let's
just say that containers

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.060
are a mechanism of deployment.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.150
So once you have that container,
what do you do with it?

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.380
How do you run it?

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:54.050
If I have a container,
what do I do with it?

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:57.850
And the beauty of containers is
that it doesn't really matter.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.900
How the container gets ran,
how it scales up or down,

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.040
shouldn't concern you.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.100
It's just a matter of
you have your software,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.880
it's in a container, and some
other orchestration layer

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.670
takes care of running
it on machines.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:16.090
So if we look at the
Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.950
you can see all
of the options you

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.110
have for running containers.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.630
Let's go from left to right,
starting with Google Compute

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.100
Engine.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.220
Google Compute Engine is
infrastructure as a service.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.150
You have a VM.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.340
It's close to the bare metal.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.910
You can do whatever
you want with that VM,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.400
and therein lies all the
power and capabilities.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.700
You can define how your
containers are ran,

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.710
what orchestration
layer you're using.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:42.910
It's wonderful.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.770
It does come with
a serious drawback,

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.320
however, in that it
gives you all this power,

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.870
and you have to decide how
your software is running

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:51.470
and how to orchestrate it.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.030
And so it can be kind of a pain.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:57.240
But if you go to Google
Container Engine,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:02.040
the unit of deployment there
isn't just a VM, but rather

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.590
how to run a series
of containers

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:09.330
and expose it in a cluster,
organized as internal services

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:10.950
or external services.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:15.870
You're defining how software--
you give it your containers

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.260
and you define how they should
be scaled up or rolled out,

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.280
but not necessarily how
they should be scheduled,

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.100
which specific VM
should run which

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.650
specific container, and so on.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.429
And so some of these
capabilities of load balancing

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:37.140
or rollout are handled by
Google Compute Engine, which

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.070
is implemented essentially
as a Google-specific

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:45.450
version of Kubernetes,
an open source container

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.790
orchestration software
that you'll probably hear

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.340
a lot about at this conference.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.740
And finally, there's
Google App Engine,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.890
which is the traditional
platform as a service.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.200
In the past, App
Engine would give you

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.860
a lot of great benefits
such as great API's to use,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.750
automatic scaling, and so on.

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.570
The drawback was
that you had to run

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.330
your software in
a special sandbox

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.460
that App Engine would then use.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:13.860
And the biggest drawback,
at least for me,

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.310
was that you couldn't run
.NET apps on App Engine.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:22.010
That was, of course, until App
Engine Flexible Environment.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.640
That's a feature of App
Engine that takes a container

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.130
and runs that, scales
it up, provides many

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.100
of the other benefits of
App Engine, the platform

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.390
as a service, but you control
the software that's being ran.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:38.990
You can specify whatever
VM or software you want.

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:46.370
So today we have launched a .NET
core container that can be ran

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:50.090
on Google Container Engine
or Google App Engine Flexible

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:53.600
Environment, so that you
can take .NET applications,

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.360
run them on the .NET
core infrastructure,

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:58.940
and scale them out
on Google's cloud.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.780
So for our talk, we're going
to focus on primarily Container

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.240
Engine and App Engine.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:11.090
So now let's start to look
at how we actually go about

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:14.000
building an application,
a .NET application,

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.920
and putting it on
Google's cloud.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.320
Could we switch to the
demo machine, please?

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.210
All right, so I'm going
to talk about two tools--

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.210
Cloud Tools for Visual Studio,
and Cloud Tools for PowerShell.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.460
Whenever there's a new
technology, or framework,

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:29.660
or something, it's great.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.850
But you've got to learn
all these new tools

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:35.330
and these new idioms, and
it's just a road bump.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:37.719
And so for Cloud Tools
for Visual Studio

00:10:37.719 --> 00:10:39.260
and Cloud Tools for
PowerShell, we're

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.080
just trying to provide
extensions for the tools

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:43.670
you already use and love.

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:48.500
So John's going to open up
the Cloud Explore, which

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.570
is part of our Visual
Studio extension

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:55.400
that allows you to browse
your cloud platform resources.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:58.370
We launched this in the
middle of last year,

00:10:58.370 --> 00:10:59.900
and then we've just
continually been

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.170
adding more and more resource
browsers and visualizers

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:04.530
within our extension.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.350
With the long term
goal of just allowing

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.640
you to manage a lot of your
resources from within the IDE

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.530
and not needing to switch
between the Google Cloud

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:16.474
console, your development
environment, and so on.

00:11:16.474 --> 00:11:18.140
And if you're wondering
where to get it,

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:22.370
you can just go to the
Manage Extensions and Updates

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:26.210
page in Visual Studio, and
just search for Google Cloud.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:29.540
And important to
note, we are running

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.930
with the latest and greatest
version of the extension which

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:34.817
just came out today.

00:11:34.817 --> 00:11:36.650
JOHN SKEET: And I
installed it this morning.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.441
CHRIS SMITH: Right.

00:11:37.441 --> 00:11:38.990
So if you type
really, really fast,

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.170
you can run through the
same demo on your laptop,

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:44.000
