00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:45.380
CHRIS SMITH: Yeah.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:46.088
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:12:46.088 --> 00:12:47.900
We have more than 10.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.930
JOHN SKEET: We'll just
say there's a lot.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:53.510
CHRIS SMITH: Now,
for our application,

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.940
JudgeAProgrammin
gBookByItsCover.com,

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:59.510
we are storing the book covers
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.830
So let's see what buckets we
have on Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.350
We can use the
Get-GcsBucket command.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:12.160
And Cloud Storage
is going to return

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:15.190
the strongly-typed
bucket objects.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.170
These aren't just
string representations,

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:20.500
these are objects that have
time stamps, names, other pieces

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:21.610
of metadata.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:24.250
And so you can see
in addition to where

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:26.500
we're hosting, say, the
containers and other pieces

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.100
of data, the
prog-book-covers Bucket,

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.300
where all the data is stored.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.800
Now, we can continue to use
these commandlets as objects

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.620
or whatnot, but
conceptually, I think

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:43.810
of cloud storage as a really,
really, really, big hard drive.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:46.150
And one of the
benefits of PowerShell

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:51.130
is you can create providers to
treat non-file system resources

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:54.370
as file systems or something.

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.180
Why don't you just show it off?

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.300
JOHN SKEET: So all we
need to do is just change

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:02.340
directory to prog-book-covers.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.980
And we can just see what's
in there as if we were just

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:08.240
browsing a local hard disk,
and then we could look in, say,

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:12.390
covers, and then look
in covers//large.

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.450
CHRIS SMITH: And use the
common basic PowerShell

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:19.230
commands, and it'll behave as
if it is a local file system.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.565
Which, to be clear, it is not.

00:14:20.565 --> 00:14:23.910
Not everything is supported
in the PowerShell provider.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.300
But if you're just trying to
navigate or browse some data,

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.610
it's much more
productive than, say,

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.580
using these other
standard commandlets.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:35.700
But just so we don't spend
too much time on PowerShell,

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.100
we'll show off one
more commandlet,

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.980
which is how to
pipeline these results.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:44.430
So by composing different
PowerShell commandlets,

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:48.002
we can do some advanced
transformer query--

00:14:48.002 --> 00:14:49.710
JOHN SKEET: Can I stop
