00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:50.030
So we're using HttpClient
to make this request.

00:34:50.030 --> 00:34:52.340
And HttpClient is
built so that you

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:57.830
can hook in your own listeners,
and it's very, very simple.

00:34:57.830 --> 00:35:02.870
In my JSON back-end, which is
very simple code, all we do

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:08.650
is we create an HttpClient
from a Trace Header Propagating

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:12.430
Handler, which is a
scary name for what you

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.530
want to put into an HttpClient.

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:18.000
And that gets just injected
by dependency injection

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:21.090
as is entirely idiomatic
for ASP.NET Core.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:23.770
Have you spotted some
kind of pattern here?

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:26.260
We want to be very much
doing the right thing

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:28.550
for the platform that
we're working with.

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:30.590
So you create an HttpClient.

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:32.740
And then when the
front-end says,

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:37.120
OK, I'm going to create a trace
with an ID of blah blah blah.

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:41.920
Then when you make any HTTP
request with that HttpClient,

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:45.160
it's automatically going to add
a header that the equivalent

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:49.660
code down in the
back-end is going

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:52.510
to say, ah, I see you've
already got that Trace Header.

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:55.070
So therefore I will
make sure that when

00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:57.850
I do any tracing to say
that I've got the request,

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.670
or any manual tracing
that you're doing,

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:01.674
it all attaches it together.

00:36:01.674 --> 00:36:03.340
Which means you can
see what's going on.

