00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:33.690
And this is all
slightly influxed.

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:36.590
We're really trying to make
things as easy as we possibly

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:38.150
can.

00:36:38.150 --> 00:36:43.310
But all we need say is, oh,
just run this action here.

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:46.040
So inserting a vote
into Datastore,

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:50.360
just run that in a Trace span
called DatastoreInsertVote.

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:54.080
And, for example,
when we have the--

00:36:54.080 --> 00:36:57.350
doing the votes, we need to
do two different Datastore

00:36:57.350 --> 00:36:58.340
operations.

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:02.360
We look up a summary of
things until recently,

00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:08.110
and then we do a separate query.

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.670
So we call RunInSpan again.

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:13.190
And if I go back to
our cheating Trace,

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:15.070
I'll see right at the bottom--

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:18.010
oh, no, I think I
ran out of space.

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:22.580
But I think if I look
for stats and try

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:28.698
to find it from back
when it wasn't cheating--

00:37:28.698 --> 00:37:32.510
is that a Stats one?

00:37:32.510 --> 00:37:35.250
I'm not sure why it's
not filtering to stats.

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:35.750
OK.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:36.520
There we go.

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:40.290
So back when it wasn't cheating,
so it was a lot quicker.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:43.320
We can see that we
have DataStoreSummary,

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:45.450
and we know that that
took 14 milliseconds

00:37:45.450 --> 00:37:46.890
and DataStoreQueryVotes.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.260
And these happen to be
doing Datastore things.

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.099
But the point is that it's only
because I called RunInSpan.

00:37:52.099 --> 00:37:53.640
You could be doing
whatever you want.

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:56.220
You could be talking to
completely different services

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:59.910
not even on Google, or doing
your own internal computation,

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:02.540
or calling your
InServices, whatever it is.

00:38:02.540 --> 00:38:03.900
These can nest.

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:08.010
It's all beautiful to see what's
going on within your requests.

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:10.734
So that's one aspect of
Stackdriver Diagnostics.

00:38:10.734 --> 00:38:12.150
But I've already
sort of shown you

00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:14.400
the code to do two
other things, which

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:16.940
is Error Reporting and Logging.

00:38:16.940 --> 00:38:19.050
I will briefly show you.

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:21.640
People generally know
what Logging looks like.

00:38:21.640 --> 00:38:24.060
So I probably won't
go into that other

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:28.080
than to say it's probably richer
Logging than you're used to.

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:30.210
This is because Google
has a lot of experience

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:33.990
of, you don't want to be looking
through 100 different log

00:38:33.990 --> 00:38:36.630
files for one particular
thing just because you've

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:39.060
got two replicated
services, each

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:41.820
with 50 different replicas.

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:43.590
In general, Google
Cloud Platform

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:46.310
tries to build on the
experience that Google's got

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:48.210
of-- we know how
to build software,

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:50.400
and we know the problems
of building software.

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:52.560
So I think you'll
find the Logging

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:54.440
richer than you may be used to.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:56.400
But the Error Reporting--

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:59.190
obviously, my code normally
doesn't throw any exceptions,

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:01.230
but Chris's code sometimes does.

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:06.410
So I've had to explicitly
add some breaking things

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:08.730
into our application.

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:14.430
So we do have two extra URLs
that you may not be expecting.

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:20.620
One of GoBang that throws
an exception directly.

00:39:20.620 --> 00:39:23.040
And the other one
is GoBangBackend,

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:25.720
that makes a request
to the back-end,

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:28.180
and that has another
control that will GoBang.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:32.410
So let's just visit
both of those URLs.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.360
So if we go to
GoBang, you'll see

00:39:34.360 --> 00:39:38.900
I've got the entirely vanilla
ASP.NET Core error page.

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:40.540
Let's hit it a few
times to prove--

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:42.915
CHRIS SMITH: Now, I'd like to
point out that that code is

00:39:42.915 --> 00:39:44.220
by John Skeet, first author.

00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:45.960
[JOHN LAUGHS]

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:48.790
JOHN SKEET: And we
can do GoBangBackend.

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:52.290
And this time it's not
actually a failure response,

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:53.920
HTTP response from
the front-end,

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:56.930
because we expected it to fail.

00:39:56.930 --> 00:39:59.060
But now that we've
had those happen,

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:01.010
and we can make them
happen a few times,

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:05.320
we can go to Stackdriver
Error Reporting.

00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:07.720
And we can see not just those,
but everything else that's

00:40:07.720 --> 00:40:09.670
happened.

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:12.760
So we can see, OK, we've
got this back-end failure,

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.310
and we can see that it's
happened a few times recently.

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:19.190
And once when we were
demoing this in prep earlier,

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:21.550
and once yesterday
when we were prepping.

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:24.340
We've got the Bangety
Bang Bang Bang,

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:26.110
which is the front-end one.

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:28.030
We've got
KeyNotFoundException, which

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:30.446
is Chris Smith not knowing the
proper use of Get-hashcode.

00:40:30.446 --> 00:40:31.840
[CHRIS LAUGHS]

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:34.720
I kid you not, and I'm happy
to give more details later

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:37.810
on in private.

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:40.080
But we can see it all.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:43.600
We have the Stack
Trace right there.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:47.640
So you can find out what is
going on in your application

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:49.740
very easily, and
that really was--

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:52.560
tracing, we had to add
a few different things.

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.920
For Error Reporting,
it really was as simple

00:40:55.920 --> 00:41:00.715
as use Google Exception Logging.

00:41:00.715 --> 00:41:02.090
The only thing I
would say is you

00:41:02.090 --> 00:41:05.780
need to make sure you put
it in the right place.

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:09.050
Previously, I had it
there, at which point

00:41:09.050 --> 00:41:12.080
it did entirely different
things because of the way

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:14.300
that the middleware
of ASP.NET Core works.

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:17.390
So if you run into problems
with Error Reporting,

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:20.120
make sure that your code looks
exactly like our sample code.

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:22.370
Well, that's the only little
detail I'll give in that.

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:22.800
CHRIS SMITH: And we should--

00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:24.350
JOHN SKEET: But it's easy.

00:41:24.350 --> 00:41:26.450
Well, that's the way
that middleware works.

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:30.180
So the Use Exception Handler
there is going to say,

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:31.910
well, I'll just
render the Razor View,

00:41:31.910 --> 00:41:33.990
and there's no
longer an exception.

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:35.799
So if we get pulled
after that, we

00:41:35.799 --> 00:41:37.090
haven't got anything to report.

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:38.131
CHRIS SMITH: Fair enough.

00:41:38.131 --> 00:41:40.130
JOHN SKEET: It's fine.

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:43.490
So with Stackdriver Logging,
Stackdriver Error Reporting,

00:41:43.490 --> 00:41:47.030
and Stackdriver Tracing, there's
one aspect to Stackdriver

00:41:47.030 --> 00:41:48.770
that I haven't even
mentioned before,

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:50.840
and that's forensic debugging.

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:53.990
I don't have anything to show
you on that front just yet.

00:41:53.990 --> 00:41:56.280
But maybe watch this space.

00:41:56.280 --> 00:41:57.800
CHRIS SMITH: Boom.

00:41:57.800 --> 00:42:01.290
Could we cut back to
the slides, please?

00:42:01.290 --> 00:42:01.790
OK.

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:07.670
So we did the super-slick
demo, awe-inspiring demo,

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:12.470
death-defying demo,
mind-blowing demo.

00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:15.760
OK, great, no more demos.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:17.170
Tear.

00:42:17.170 --> 00:42:21.910
So we covered quite a few
products, many tools, and so

00:42:21.910 --> 00:42:25.150
let's just recap what
we've covered today.

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:28.480
First, we showed Cloud Tools for
Visual Studio and Cloud Tools

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:32.650
for PowerShell as extensions to
the tools that you're probably

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:35.300
using today to build
.NET applications.

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:37.600
And these are just ways
to help make it easier

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.500
to transition your
applications to Google's cloud.

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:44.050
Then we covered containers.

00:42:44.050 --> 00:42:49.780
Today we launched the ASP.NET
Core container image that you

00:42:49.780 --> 00:42:53.800
can then use as a base image
to run .NET applications

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:58.600
on Google's Cloud Platform,
such as App Engine Flexible

00:42:58.600 --> 00:43:01.120
Environment, or Google
Container Engine.

00:43:01.120 --> 00:43:03.790
And both of those can
be deployment targets

00:43:03.790 --> 00:43:05.770
from within Visual Studio.

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:08.440
And then, finally, we
showed off some of the,

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:12.790
dare I say, sweet, sweet
runtime diagnostics that you

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:14.410
can get from Stackdriver.

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:17.200
This is all available from
just adding a reference

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:19.900
to an instrumentation
library and just hooking it

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:21.320
into your application.

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:25.660
You don't need to do a lot of
contorting of your application

00:43:25.660 --> 00:43:27.100
to take advantage of this.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:30.741
Rather, we want to make
it as simple as possible.

00:43:30.741 --> 00:43:31.240
All right.

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:37.770
So with that in mind, let
us find out the world's best

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.690
